#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188002 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188295 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70824c34-6527-5fe5-a86f-2ca683231f75" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Continuous Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/id #uuid "175f3917-8801-515d-a493-5f36a52aa01b" :question/math-academy-id "e-10097" :question/problem "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n![](images/c7/c73c9ecb4b2f4288cdefd5546f8ec21f46008695568318e9344eb70c88ffa334.svg)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f_Y(y) = \\dfrac{1}{2}e^{-y/2}$ for $y\\in \\left[0,\\infty\\right)$\n\nII. $f(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}x{e}^{-y/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-y/2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-y/2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y \\in \\left[0,\\infty\\right).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4}x{e}^{-y/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}x{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{b}{e}^{-y/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}x{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-2{e}^{-y/2}]}_{0}^{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}x{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[-2{e}^{-b/2}+2] \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x \\in \\left[0,2\\right].$ So, when $x \\in \\left[0,2\\right]$ and $y\\in \\left[0,\\infty\\right),$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{4}xe^{-y/2}}_{\\large f(x,y)} = \\underbrace{\\Big(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\Big)}_{\\large f_X(x)} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\Big(\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{-y/2}\\Big)}_{\\large f_Y(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188209 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188274 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8382e740-f1a5-52f6-97f5-df8c11929ef4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Joint PDF of Two Independent Random Variables Using Marginal PDFs"}] :question/id #uuid "e4007ddd-a5ed-5f3d-96cc-4c911d97a615" :question/math-academy-id "e-9187" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n![](images/ec/ecb3e3a465f5987f182ccf843562a43f1e4c65db0a7b12e8e86cd9724dfa2d7b.svg)\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{y}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}x{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y) \\in [0, 3] \\times [0, 2].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n![](images/1c/1cc8f365960775c99a6cfa56736189758207b2b35bac414e2c85324a600c2619.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188210 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188275 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee434eee-2eb0-5a0b-b4a1-1be98bf5b217" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Joint Probability for Some Independent Random Variables Using Marginal PDFs"}] :question/id #uuid "1e1f0ecb-6f45-53ac-ae5d-ff953567aefa" :question/math-academy-id "e-9188" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n![](images/d5/d55ab8270e18d87100db9634a044d47e9314420674e8045a20cecd9b3da5542e.svg)\n\nFind $P(X \\geq 3, Y \\leq  2).$" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}y \\\\  & =\\frac{5y}{6{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y) \\in \\left[2,5\\right]\\times \\left[1,3\\right].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n![](images/eb/eb900092e22a38e1bbc0b26853647353d5d21fc0f46c56842525e9b11d5392a1.svg)\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3,Y\\le 2) & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{5y}{6{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{5}{6{x}^{2}}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{5}{6{x}^{2}}(\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{5}{4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{5}{4x}]}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188274 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8382e740-f1a5-52f6-97f5-df8c11929ef4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Joint PDF of Two Independent Random Variables Using Marginal PDFs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2972b8aa-250f-5bd2-8165-85701bb5ae9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Impossible to determine using only the given information." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185557} {:answer/id #uuid "1b5b791a-0329-5126-9371-73ede1f87407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}{y}^{3}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477783} {:answer/id #uuid "8049d7d6-f564-5f57-bb96-831a621ad9c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478002} {:answer/id #uuid "87ca1649-49ab-574f-a99b-ac6d040531af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{4{y}^{2}}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478011} {:answer/id #uuid "fa496f92-afc6-5834-904b-3507c2afd467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2972b8aa-250f-5bd2-8165-85701bb5ae9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Impossible to determine using only the given information." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185557} :answer-field/id #uuid "8245d5cc-a0d3-5155-a8be-2a54d7d8e0a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b3e96d9d-5e09-5192-9419-9c5668ecfb87" :question/math-academy-id "q-136861" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{3}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$\n\n*Hint: We do not know if $X$ and $Y$ are independent.*" :question/worked-solution "Since we don't know whether $X$ and $Y$ are independent, it is impossible to determine the joint distribution of $X$ and $Y$ using only the given information."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188295 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70824c34-6527-5fe5-a86f-2ca683231f75" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Continuous Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fe78ca8-334b-531b-a512-a341ac492a5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185563} {:answer/id #uuid "c4be7868-6891-5064-82ca-d0abcd776c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185564} {:answer/id #uuid "fcbd88c7-a64d-54bd-9593-2d1a47d427e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185565} {:answer/id #uuid "2262bddb-2ca0-514c-9db6-75b84a2dfe4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185566} {:answer/id #uuid "7afee5a8-f42b-5860-a3d2-673f5458e48c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4be7868-6891-5064-82ca-d0abcd776c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185564} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9a62c9a-789e-53e7-a2b4-febcf8bd0baf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e49ff8a6-6b90-594a-be32-eb49510ed619" :question/math-academy-id "q-136864" :question/problem "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{X}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}$ for $x\\in [0,1]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{[{x}^{2}y+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,1].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [0,1].$ So, when $x\\in [0,1]$ and $y\\in [0,1],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}\\ne {{(\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{(\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is *not* true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478431 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188274 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8382e740-f1a5-52f6-97f5-df8c11929ef4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Joint PDF of Two Independent Random Variables Using Marginal PDFs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d389b13-cfd3-5648-b491-bd03bbd0f6fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}x(y+1)^{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477789} {:answer/id #uuid "482bddba-7826-5a7a-9481-6318c2041403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Impossible to determine using only the given information." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477877} {:answer/id #uuid "613da1a0-2c7c-5b40-8004-729f27c2a407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}x(y+1), & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477933} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc289fe-8cd8-534c-be91-cf8031232756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}(x+1), & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478109} {:answer/id #uuid "d58623b4-ab17-5950-b533-644d2efc520c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}(y+1), & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d58623b4-ab17-5950-b533-644d2efc520c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}(y+1), & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478158} :answer-field/id #uuid "179aa336-60c2-5460-a3dc-99b5cf0016ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9efe9c4-330f-5e24-830e-56ad2ba97826" :question/math-academy-id "q-143342" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}(y+1), & \\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{2})\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}(y+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}(y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,2]\\times [0,1].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}(y+1), & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188274 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8382e740-f1a5-52f6-97f5-df8c11929ef4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Joint PDF of Two Independent Random Variables Using Marginal PDFs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2111ac82-07a6-57fc-92e2-4359e8908bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477797} {:answer/id #uuid "4d64756e-fe2f-54b7-b56f-ba3b17d7bc15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}x\\sqrt{y}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477895} {:answer/id #uuid "67ff2263-d24d-5772-b801-d642d3b899df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}y\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477945} {:answer/id #uuid "9cb9fa73-242d-54e2-9813-05078b917a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3y\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478037} {:answer/id