{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(x+1){e}^{y}, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477939} {:answer/id #uuid "7af45ab1-82da-5803-966f-cc3a6b56e690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}({y}^{2}+1){e}^{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477988} {:answer/id #uuid "7eafcf78-0a55-5493-885a-ef84c05d0caa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}({x}^{2}+1){e}^{2y}, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477997} {:answer/id #uuid "ccdd4ec2-219e-59c6-aacc-1ad870c4924d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}({y}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c3eb513-f277-57b1-a8f8-5d539a198456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Impossible to determine using only the given information." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477922} :answer-field/id #uuid "1163d6a2-1bf2-5d52-8be1-3f994d864230" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "321896e4-7ca4-574a-a1b1-f3375d212e65" :question/math-academy-id "q-143351" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{2x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{10}({y}^{2}+1), & \\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$\n\n*Hint: We do not know if $X$ and $Y$ are independent.*" :question/worked-solution "Since we don't know whether $X$ and $Y$ are independent, it is impossible to determine the joint distribution of $X$ and $Y$ using only the given information."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478423 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188275 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee434eee-2eb0-5a0b-b4a1-1be98bf5b217" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Joint Probability for Some Independent Random Variables Using Marginal PDFs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a206236-0e7e-5bbc-8d9f-f1ab1aa63362" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477753} {:answer/id #uuid "147e0940-a76d-5258-82a8-9ce0853f0d75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477771} {:answer/id #uuid "69fc415e-381a-5926-85eb-1f8964653882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477950} {:answer/id #uuid "6e0499d6-0793-579a-b3ca-91fa82d7407c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477963} {:answer/id #uuid "7f62311f-2802-59c1-89f9-ddd0175dfae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a206236-0e7e-5bbc-8d9f-f1ab1aa63362" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477753} :answer-field/id #uuid "562abd85-92be-5ffd-a029-9f990daee18d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8523bc2d-4a4c-583b-88c5-0e1cec2367ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-143355" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}, & \\,0\\le x, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y}, & \\,0\\le y, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(0\\le X,Y\\le 3).$" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\cdot {e}^{-y} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x-y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,\\infty )\\times [0,\\infty ).$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x-y}, & \\,0\\le x,\\,0\\le y \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X,Y\\le 3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-x-y}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-y}{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[{\\int }_{0}^{b}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-y}{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[-{e}^{-x}]_{0}^{b}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[-{e}^{-y}]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478394 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188275 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee434eee-2eb0-5a0b-b4a1-1be98bf5b217" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Joint Probability for Some Independent Random Variables Using Marginal PDFs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d546f32-ef09-541b-97ac-be58cb5dc44f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477758} {:answer/id #uuid "45d86a13-2930-5fbe-9f9e-208e3a7528ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477868} {:answer/id #uuid "4b6558e5-634d-558a-88ea-d6cf86b16af0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477885} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ee63da-8fcd-5b05-a984-8fe6adf18a79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478119} {:answer/id #uuid "dbcc6282-6b95-51b0-a9d8-b8431a9541e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b6558e5-634d-558a-88ea-d6cf86b16af0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477885} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dfc71ab-b965-55c6-8fd6-517e70644c0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "418cd51b-54c6-5702-b58c-50620816a1ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-143356" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}(x+1), & \\,2\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}, & \\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\ge 3,Y\\le 4).$" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(x+1)\\cdot \\frac{2}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+1}{4{y}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [2,4]\\times [1,2].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{4{y}^{2}}, & \\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3,Y\\le 4) & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{x+1}{4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{x+1}{4}{[-\\frac{1}{y}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{x+1}{4}(\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{x+1}{8}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{8}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x)]}_{3}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(8+4)-\\frac{1}{8}(\\frac{9}{2}+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188275 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee434eee-2eb0-5a0b-b4a1-1be98bf5b217" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Joint Probability for Some Independent Random Variables Using Marginal PDFs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "026d0ea1-9366-5664-b4b9-994b0042a77c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477738} {:answer/id #uuid "31b68073-6689-585d-ae4a-b07f5e178bbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477827} {:answer/id #uuid "546d128e-cde5-59e1-ab9e-a78e7cc369cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477912} {:answer/id #uuid "72d5367b-b862-518e-8884-551d2a8c05d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477979} {:answer/id #uuid "c20a011c-44e2-5773-889d-7b1fc1a3e892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "546d128e-cde5-59e1-ab9e-a78e7cc369cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477912} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9f9d110-27a8-57fc-9bdb-1973382778ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "427c86d9-8f85-5921-9341-2ec04eff93eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-143358" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}x, & \\,0\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}y, & \\,2\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(1\\le X\\le 2,1\\le Y\\le 3).$" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}x\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}xy,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,4]\\times [2,3].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{20}xy, & \\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,2\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le X\\le 2,1\\le Y\\le 3) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{20}xy\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{20}x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{2}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{8}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(1)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188275 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee434eee-2eb0-5a0b-b4a1-1be98bf5b217" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Joint Probability for Some Independent Random Variables Using Marginal PDFs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3915f74c-160e-5152-b96a-aa964c8e6d28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.289" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477842} {:answer/id #uuid "41191433-d83d-5aef-b9ef-6c0eafed2ba4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.423" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477858} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d0fc68-fa7c-5c6f-94a5-1a96c8da2fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.382" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478056} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c6e5e8-9170-5a8e-b1b3-f5e944fbbc70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.278" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478183} {:answer/id #uuid "eb3d7459-2b62-5aa7-8e9a-444810f04b91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.365" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb3d7459-2b62-5aa7-8e9a-444810f04b91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.365" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478202} :answer-field/id #uuid "543f86a7-9d04-5010-8199-ce0c0b78789a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22625d61-74d2-5702-9e7b-d31919b31ad0" :question/math-academy-id "q-143361" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}x, & \\,1\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 1.5,Y\\ge 1).$ Round your answer to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}x\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [1,2]\\times [0,2].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{2}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1.5,Y\\ge 1) & ={\\int }_{1}^{1.5}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{1.5}\\frac{1}{4}x{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{1.5}\\frac{1}{4}x(\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{1.5}\\frac{7}{12}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{7{x}^{2}}{24}]}_{1}^{1.5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{7(1.5)^{2}}{24}-\\frac{7(1)^{2}}{24} \\\\  & ≈0.365.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478424 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188275 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee434eee-2eb0-5a0b-b4a1-1be98bf5b217" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Joint Probability for Some Independent Random Variables Using Marginal PDFs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06de0808-d75c-5542-8785-2b794c445fff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477747} {:answer/id #uuid "51924fc7-2fd0-5328-9944-f6e3cdb40a44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{48}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477902} {:answer/id #uuid "95c29e5c-b4a7-5d82-979c-afede81345d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478028} {:answer/id #uuid "c4fbbc9c-012e-5a54-abce-85136bc47289" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{48}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478125} {:answer/id #uuid "c98309c8-400d-5a8e-9816-a2efbfa6aa24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{48}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c98309c8-400d-5a8e-9816-a2efbfa6aa24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{48}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478133} :answer-field/id #uuid "66f0a460-84c7-54c5-b966-be06a0c78634" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "85b259be-6bf2-5d1d-9133-ee3e5619e62d" :question/math-academy-id "q-143367" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}(x+2), & \\,1\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{y}, & \\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡4, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(1\\le X\\le 2,Y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(x+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}(x+2){e}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [1,3]\\times [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡4].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{24}(x+2){e}^{y}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le X\\le 2,Y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{1}{24}(x+2){e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{24}(x+2)[{e}^{y}]_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{24}(x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{24}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2x)]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}(2+4)-\\frac{1}{24}(\\frac{1}{2}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{5}{48} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{48}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478421 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188295 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70824c34-6527-5fe5-a86f-2ca683231f75" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Continuous Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e826808-605f-5120-948a-59aa92c12a76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477791} {:answer/id #uuid "1fa102a5-c5aa-52f0-989d-f63963d7b079" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477794} {:answer/id #uuid "a9b93fcd-32b7-5a8a-ba02-6b06a9ee6a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478061} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f0a92b-fef0-5f9a-987b-f4a0d5aba13f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478132} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ba1b0c-7cb3-592b-be9b-86271440aa5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9b93fcd-32b7-5a8a-ba02-6b06a9ee6a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478061} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2390c11-a776-5a6c-9a83-24a35d5a12f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7b80fc5d-3cc1-506f-9e31-9f2ad06b5cc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-143375" :question/problem "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{X}(x)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot [y]_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi },\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$ So, when $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ and $y\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}={{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{(\\frac{4}{\\pi })}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188295 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70824c34-6527-5fe5-a86f-2ca683231f75" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Continuous Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2977e316-273e-5445-b50a-cb57603ee29b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477813} {:answer/id #uuid "72c2b192-0559-53f5-881a-0579227ab7be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477978} {:answer/id #uuid "a54f7dfd-699f-5d20-82dd-a0e20cd154bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478052} {:answer/id #uuid "a68447ae-8b57-5690-8d88-53f52127910e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478055} {:answer/id #uuid "cddc4fa5-ede2-5454-8d85-97bd80342cdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cddc4fa5-ede2-5454-8d85-97bd80342cdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478144} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c133fae-f0bc-5d33-b131-23842ea13a8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eac65ba4-c3fd-5292-817a-bbb6eafdc1fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-143379" :question/problem "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y{e}^{-3x},\\, & 0\\le x,\\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{X}(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}$ for $x\\in [0,\\infty )$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2y{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{e}^{-3x}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-3x}(2-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-3x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2y{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2y{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{b}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2y{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}]}_{0}^{b} \\\\  & =2y{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3b}+\\frac{1}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [1,2].$ So, when $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $y\\in [1,2],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{2y{e}^{-3x}}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}={{(3{e}^{-3x})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{(\\frac{2}{3}y)}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188295 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70824c34-6527-5fe5-a86f-2ca683231f75" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Continuous Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "119315f1-83ce-5a6d-bbc2-c00d428fdecd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477764} {:answer/id #uuid "19b24d2b-c4be-5498-9ef9-469fe4b09f1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477781} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc8938b-fd54-51cb-aba9-d1d1f78e12d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478041} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e7b647-652d-5261-83d8-0b43e4647da7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478151} {:answer/id #uuid "e459d44b-76eb-50a7-9c70-6772fdb63601" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dc8938b-fd54-51cb-aba9-d1d1f78e12d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478041} :answer-field/id #uuid "847b995d-d2da-58ee-9d30-8b4bd5b80731" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b7f5fbd8-1657-5933-82eb-1254d5fe2e87" :question/math-academy-id "q-143380" :question/problem "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{8}(xy)^{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{Y}(y)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}$ for $y\\in [0,2]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{9}{8}(xy)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}{y}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}{y}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [0,2].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{9}{8}(xy)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}{x}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}{x}^{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,1].$ So, when $x\\in [0,1]$ and $y\\in [0,2],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{\\frac{9}{8}(xy)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}={{(3{x}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{(\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188563 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188295 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70824c34-6527-5fe5-a86f-2ca683231f75" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Continuous Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95f313db-cd02-59f4-aa9c-cb2b1e94ddf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185659} {:answer/id #uuid "993dafd1-cd1f-5129-b778-e3d2650159e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185660} {:answer/id #uuid "fbed530d-6837-5d0b-b58e-2aa223dedb02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185661} {:answer/id #uuid "3512c16c-3729-51fc-a4e5-efb4b1b9d5aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185662} {:answer/id #uuid "6dc005ec-f9c6-5f8a-a03a-55519f377e28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3512c16c-3729-51fc-a4e5-efb4b1b9d5aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185662} :answer-field/id #uuid "665dc965-8c73-559b-83c8-3ab6995229d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af8d6c3e-9622-5721-91e2-327a3d421c31" :question/math-academy-id "q-143384" :question/problem "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}x{y}^{3},\\, & 2\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{X}(x)=2x$ for $x\\in [2,4]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}x{[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [2,4].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{2}{3}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}(8-2) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [0,1].$ So, when $x\\in [2,4]$ and $y\\in [0,1],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{\\frac{2}{3}x{y}^{3}}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}={{(\\frac{1}{6}x)}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{(4{y}^{3})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}]}
